---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { indexablePage } from "../lib/metadata";

const metadata = indexablePage({
  title: "About Atrinik: A shared world, developed in the open",
  description:
    "Atrinik began in 2009 as an open-source online world. Learn about its history, its two implementations, and how creators and contributors shape it today.",
  canonicalPath: "/about/",
  socialImageId: "atrinik-then",
});
---

<Base metadata={metadata}>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About Atrinik</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Shared imagination. Open development.</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      Atrinik's creators shape its game world, and its software is developed in
      the open, where anyone can follow the work and contribute.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="history-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">From 2009 to today</p>
      <h2 id="history-heading">Where it began</h2>
      <p>
        Atrinik began in 2009 under founder and original lead{" "}
        <a href="https://zoeysr.com/">Zoey Rose</a>. The 2011 releases were a
        major era of development, not the project's origin.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="detail-grid">
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Founded in 2009</h3>
        <p>
          Zoey Rose founded and originally led Atrinik. She worked alongside the
          wider Atrinik Development Team as maps, art, music, quests, dialogue,
          lore, and code grew through the work of many people over the years.
        </p>
        <a
          class="link-arrow"
          href="https://github.com/atrinik/classic/commit/6e5ee3ceb53c8ce3354acd6de08a308fdd21b8da"
        >
          See the initial 2009 source <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Credit across lineages</h3>
        <p>
          Atrinik's founding sources credited Daimonin and Crossfire. Classic
          also retains work from Atrinik contributors and appropriately licensed
          upstream authors. Their repository notices and per-file records—not a
          blanket claim—remain authoritative.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/classic">
          Read the Classic history and notices <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Creative direction</h3>
        <p>
          Atrinik's maps, quests, dialogue, lore, art, animation, music, and
          sound are shaped by its creators, who review what goes into the game.
          Upstream work keeps its own author, source, license, and attribution;
          it is not automatically Atrinik-original.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="/licenses/">
          Follow the exact ownership boundaries{" "}
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="two-paths-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Two paths through the same world</p>
      <h2 id="two-paths-heading">Playable history and an MIT future</h2>
      <p>
        The two lines share a project and a setting, but they are separate
        implementations with different licenses and release readiness.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="split-grid">
      <article class="path-card path-card--classic">
        <div class="path-card-topline">
          <span class="path-number">Path I</span>
          <span class="status-tag status-tag--live">
            Playable and maintained
          </span>
        </div>
        <h3>Classic</h3>
        <p>
          The current playable line is Atrinik's maintained GPL C lineage,
          actively coordinated in the unified <code>atrinik/classic</code>{" "}
          repository. Its client, server, editor, libatrinik, and protocol share
          one version, release notes stream, and coordinated artifact set under
          GPL-2.0-or-later, with frequent 5.x releases.
        </p>
        <p>
          The client is a portable Windows download and, since October 2026, a
          Linux AppImage for x86_64 systems with glibc 2.39 or newer, such as
          Ubuntu 24.04 and later. It renders on the GPU through Direct3D 12 on
          Windows and Vulkan on Linux. There is no macOS client.
        </p>
        <ul class="path-details">
          <li>Current playable implementation</li>
          <li>Windows and Linux client downloads</li>
          <li>GPL-2.0-or-later software</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card-actions">
          <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/classic">
            Explore Atrinik Classic on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="path-card path-card--future">
        <div class="path-card-topline">
          <span class="path-number">Path II</span>
          <span class="status-tag">In development</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Next generation</h3>
        <p>
          The replacement is an MIT reimplementation and improvement: a Go
          server, a Rust client, editor, and shared renderer, a content toolkit,
          and a new Protobuf protocol. Its repositories use evidence-gated
          provenance: independent implementation is the default, while an exact,
          separable historical Classic contribution may be reused at an MIT
          destination only when a complete-history audit proves it fits one
          applicable grant's past-contribution and sole-authorship scope. The
          Classic repository remains GPL-distributed. These foundations do not
          yet form a complete game release or replace the playable Classic line.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>
            <time datetime="2026-10">October 2026</time> status:
          </strong>{" "}
          the client, server, protocol, renderer, editor, and content toolkit
          each publish independent releases, and a new Go pathfinding and
          navigation service is in early development without a release yet.
          These are tested foundations, not yet a playable game.
        </p>
        <ul class="path-details">
          <li>MIT-licensed Go and Rust foundations</li>
          <li>Independently released components</li>
          <li>Not yet available as a complete game</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card-actions">
          <a
            class="link-arrow"
            href="https://github.com/atrinik/atrinik/issues/168"
          >
            Follow the replacement roadmap <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="open-development-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">How the project works</p>
      <h2 id="open-development-heading">
        Creator-directed, developed in the open
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="detail-grid">
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Creator-directed world</h3>
        <p>
          People set the creative direction for maps, archetypes, stories,
          dialogue, graphics, animation, music, and sound effects. Every asset
          keeps its credit and license record.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Open development</h3>
        <p>
          Source, plans, issues, and release notes live in public GitHub
          repositories. Changes go through documented ownership, provenance
          review, tests, and repository validation before they ship.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Contributions are welcome</h3>
        <p>
          Anyone can report a problem, suggest an idea, or contribute code or
          content. Every change passes the same review, evidence-gated
          provenance, licensing, and validation checks.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="follow-heading">
    <aside class="callout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Follow the journey</p>
        <h2 id="follow-heading">See what is happening now</h2>
        <p>
          Visit the project repositories for current plans, source, and ways to
          contribute.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-actions">
        <a
          class="button button--primary"
          href="https://github.com/atrinik/atrinik/issues/168"
        >
          Read the roadmap
        </a>
        <a class="button button--ghost" href="https://github.com/atrinik/">
          Browse Atrinik on GitHub
        </a>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </section>
</Base>
